AB  - When a learner expresses frustration, a conversational tutor can ignore it, apologize, or acknowledge the feeling and reframe the difficulty. In a Wizard-of-Oz study with 96 participants solving logic puzzles, we compared these three response styles. Acknowledge-and-reframe increased the number of puzzles attempted after a failure by 28% relative to ignoring, while apologizing had no effect on persistence and lowered perceived tutor competence. Participants described scripted empathy as hollow when it repeated.
